          "text": "{\"relation\":\"MATCH\",\"consensus_summary\":\"The almond primarily signifies divine watchfulness and prompt fulfillment of God’s word in Jeremiah, while other recognized contextual associations include God’s authentication of Aaronic priesthood and the almond-shaped cups of the sanctuary lampstand.\",\"book_summary\":\"The almond is the wakeful or watcher-tree, a sign of the chosen Levitical priesthood through Aaron’s fruitful rod, and the floral form used for the sanctuary lamps.\",\"consensus_core_identification\":\"Almond imagery denotes divine watchfulness, with established secondary applications to authenticated priesthood and sanctuary light.\",\"book_core_identification\":\"Almond imagery denotes watchfulness and authenticated Levitical priesthood, and is incorporated into the sanctuary lampstand.\",\"core_relation\":\"EQUIVALENT\",\"extension_relation\":\"SAME_CASES\",\"boundary_disagreement\":\"No material boundary disagreement is present. The book foregrounds priesthood alongside watchfulness and mentions the lampstand, all of which the consensus already recognizes. Its omission of other contextual associations such as old age does not amount to their rejection.\",\"material_contradiction\":\"None. The book neither replaces nor denies the consensus identification.\",\"rationale\":\"After reducing both readings to their minimum claims, the book reproduces the consensus’s central watchfulness identification and two of its established contextual strands: Aaron’s divinely chosen priesthood and the almond-shaped sanctuary lampstand. Differences concern emphasis and compression rather than referent, meaning, or covered biblical cases.\",\"citation_support\":\"STRONG\",\"strongest_objection\":\"The lampstand passages describe almond-shaped cups but do not explicitly interpret their symbolic meaning, and Numbers 17:8 alone requires its surrounding context to make priestly election explicit. Even so, the printed references directly anchor all three claims.\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}"
        }
